<BaseLayout title="Driveshaft" description="Driveshaft length, yokes, and adapters for LS Genesis Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Driveshaft</h1>
  <p>
    Driveshaft length depends on engine mounts, transmission choice, and how far the powertrain sits in the bay.
    Measure with the engine and transmission mocked in final position.
  </p>

  <h2>Differential Adapter</h2>
  <p>
    Genesis diffs are not a direct LS yoke match. A common solution is a 3-bolt to 4-bolt adapter (Collins adapter is one used in the community).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Important:</strong> Only the Spicer 3-2-119 yoke has been confirmed to work with that adapter path among the options some vendors list. Other listed yokes have failed to fit correctly.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/collins-guibo-adapter.jpg" alt="Collins Toyota Lexus 3-prong guibo adapter for 1350/1310 yokes" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Collins 3-prong guibo adapter (6061 aluminum) for 1350/1310 serviceable flange yokes — used to leave the rubber isolator behind.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/driveshaft-diff-yoke.jpg" alt="Driveshaft U-joint at differential flange adapter" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Diff-side joint and adapter flange installed.
  </p>

    <h2>Builders &amp; Specs</h2>
  <p>
    Documented builds have ordered from Ron Wicks and PST (Precision Shaft Technologies) — example: 3.5&quot; aluminum shaft with a Strange front yoke.
    Always give the shop both ends' joint types and a precise length.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/driveshaft-aluminum-full.jpg" alt="Full aluminum driveshaft in shipping box" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Example finished aluminum shaft ready to install.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/driveshaft-trans-strange-yoke.jpg" alt="Strange front yoke at transmission output" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Strange front yoke at the transmission — confirm slip-yoke series and length with the shaft builder.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/driveshaft-length-measure.jpg" alt="Measuring driveshaft length at transmission yoke" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Measure with the powertrain in final position — length is critical for joint angle and slip travel.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/driveshaft-tunnel-clearance.jpg" alt="Driveshaft clearance in tunnel" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Tunnel clearance — check for contact at full suspension travel and full droop.
  </p>

  <h2>Heat Shielding (critical)</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>LS engines and headers put a lot of heat into the tunnel.</strong> Without serious shielding, you can melt carpet, overheat the cabin, damage wiring, and cook seals or the driveshaft boot.
    Plan heat control at the same time as the exhaust and driveshaft — not as an afterthought.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tunnel underside: multi-layer heat barrier (e.g. DEI-style) covering as much of the tunnel and floorpan as practical</li>
    <li>Exhaust: wrap or ceramic coating on headers and mid-pipes to keep radiant heat down</li>
    <li>Cabin side: additional barrier on the floor / tunnel from above if carpet still gets hot</li>
  </ul>

  <img src="/images/heat-shield-tunnel-underside.jpg" alt="Full tunnel heat shielding from underside of car" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Tunnel and floorpan fully lined from underneath — this is the level of coverage that keeps the cabin livable.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/heat-shield-cabin-floor.jpg" alt="Cabin floor heat shielding with barrier material (mike.built)" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Cabin-side barrier on the floor / tunnel (example from mike.built) — doubles up protection where feet and carpet sit.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/heat-shield-exhaust-wrap.jpg" alt="Heat-wrapped exhaust mid-pipes" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Wrapped mid-pipes / downpipes — reduces radiant heat into the tunnel before it ever reaches the shield.
  </p>

  <h2>Upgraded Sedan Axles</h2>
  <p>
    For higher power, some builders swap to rear axles from the Hyundai Genesis sedan (same family, often stronger or more available).
    Confirm spline counts and compressed length against your hubs and differential before ordering.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Right (passenger):</strong> GSP NCV37100 — overall compressed length ~33 2/9&quot;, 30/28 splines, 47 teeth (fits 09–11 Genesis)</li>
    <li><strong>Left (driver):</strong> GSP NCV37099 — overall compressed length ~31 3/8&quot;, 30/28 splines, 47 teeth (fits 09–11 Genesis)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Used sedan axles (e.g. 2011 Genesis sedan 3.8) also show up on the salvage market and can work if dimensions match.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/sedan-axle-right-ncv37100.jpg" alt="GSP NCV37100 rear right CV axle for 09-11 Genesis" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    NCV37100 rear right — ~33 2/9&quot; compressed length.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/sedan-axle-left-ncv37099.jpg" alt="GSP NCV37099 rear left CV axle for 09-11 Genesis" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    NCV37099 rear left — ~31 3/8&quot; compressed length.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/sedan-axle-used-left.jpg" alt="Used rear left axle from 2011 Genesis sedan" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Example used sedan left axle (2011 Genesis sedan 3.8) — verify length and splines before install.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
